# Caddyfile for Driftwood
# Replace driftwood.example.com with your actual domain
#
# Everything goes to the binary. One origin, one upstream, and the binary decides
# what answers what.
#
# The reason it has to be one origin is the try-it-out page. The control API
# refuses cross-origin requests on purpose — its allowlist is localhost only
# (internal/server/server.go, isAllowedOrigin), because it is an unauthenticated
# control plane and a wildcard there would let any page on the internet rewrite a
# running instance's proxy target. The try page drives that API, so it has to be
# same-origin with it. That is also why the site is embedded in the binary rather
# than served from a static root beside it: the alternative is widening that
# allowlist to serve static files.
#
# So: `drift --site` serves the marketing site at /, the try page at /try, and the
# dashboard and its control API under /_driftwood/*. Every other path is proxied
# to the target and sniffed, exactly as it is on a developer's machine. With
# --site off — the default — / belongs to the target like any other path, which is
# what you want when you are locally developing against an app.
#
# This file is an example for a hand-managed host. It is not what runs in
# production for this project: that is Aeroplane, which generates its own Caddy
# config and manages the domain itself. Nothing here needs editing to deploy.

driftwood.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787 {
		# /_driftwood/events is a server-sent event stream. Caddy flushes event
		# streams on its own, but only when it recognises the content type; -1
		# turns buffering off outright, which also covers the frames that arrive
		# before the first flush would have fired.
		flush_interval -1
	}

	encode gzip

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
